# Design notes — Heist display sizes

Operator-facing help stays short (tick sizes on Club Public Profile / Master Admin). Implementation detail lives here.

## Current state (s3.0.112, owner request)

| Size | Enabled | Board |
|---|---|---|
| 96×48 (`led-96x48`, biggest) | **1** | **Default** — Display 1 `display.html?location=heist-washington-dc` |
| 64×48 (`led-64x48`) | 0 | — |
| 64×32 (`led-64x32`) | 1 | Display 2 `display2.html?location=heist-washington-dc` |

Written in **both** places (they must agree):

- Firestore `clubLocations/heist-washington-dc`: `VenueSupports96x48=1`, `VenueSupports64x48=0`, `VenueSupports64x32=1`, `displayScreenFormatIds=[led-96x48, led-64x32]`, `primaryDisplayScreenFormatId=led-96x48`, `secondaryDisplayScreenFormatId=led-64x32`, `displayType`/`screenFormatId`/`ledPanel` = 96×48.
- Packaged `shared-data.js` `"heist-washington-dc"`: same values.

Why both: `display-app.js` merge gives the **packaged** `primaryDisplayScreenFormatId` / `secondaryDisplayScreenFormatId` precedence over live, and packaged `VenueSupports*` survive when Firestore has no explicit flags. `FLOQRScreenDatapoints.applyVenue` then resets the primary to the first enabled size if the primary is not enabled — so a 96×48 default with `VenueSupports96x48=0` silently falls back to 64×32.

No new display/device doc was created; only the existing venue's size fields changed. Test: `commerce-invariants.test.js` “Heist Washington DC defaults Display 1 to the 96x48 board”.

## Enabling 64×48 later

- Firestore → `VenueSupports64x48=1` and add `led-64x48` to `displayScreenFormatIds` (keep the others). Mirror in `shared-data.js`.
- Primary only changes if that board becomes Xibo Display 1.
- Xibo URL: `display.html?location=heist-washington-dc` only — never `?screen=` or `?v=`.
- **Firestore `VenueSupports*` flags win** when set.

See also `screen-datapoints.mdc`.
